A pattern normally gives a captured field its declared name. Use `as` when that name already belongs to a visible binding or another name would explain the field's role in this arm.

### Rename one payload field

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Response = {
        Err | message String |,
        Success | value String |,
    }

    response = Response::Err("oops")

    if response is {
        case Err(message as error_message): io.line(error_message)
        case Success(value as body): io.line(body)
    }
]

The first arm reads the field named `message` through the local name `error_message`. The field keeps its declared name.

### Why rename?

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    message = "outer message"

    if response is {
        case Err(message as failure_message): io.line(failure_message)
        else: io.line(message)
    }
]

The first arm logs the captured failure message. The fallback can still read the outer `message`. Moth rejects a capture that would shadow that outer binding.
